WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.760
Single-shot RAG assumes the question is a good search query,

2
00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:05.690
and that one retrieval is enough.

3
00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:09.938
Both often fail, so agentic retrieval is the standard upgrade.

4
00:00:10.738 --> 00:00:15.061
Meet Maya, an analyst at a bicycle maker, using the company assistant.

5
00:00:15.391 --> 00:00:19.416
She asks what the warranty says about cracked frames, and gets a great answer.

6
00:00:20.216 --> 00:00:22.513
Then she types: and what about the carbon one?

7
00:00:22.843 --> 00:00:25.758
Searched literally, that phrase matches nothing useful.

8
00:00:26.558 --> 00:00:28.682
So the agent first rewrites the query,

9
00:00:28.732 --> 00:00:31.372
resolving the carbon one from the chat history.

10
00:00:31.702 --> 00:00:35.172
It also splits compound questions and tries paraphrases.

11
00:00:35.972 --> 00:00:38.745
Next, it picks the right source: product docs,

12
00:00:38.795 --> 00:00:41.383
ticket history, a SQL database, or the web.

13
00:00:41.713 --> 00:00:43.562
It doesn't always hit the same index.

14
00:00:44.362 --> 00:00:48.536
Before answering, it grades the results: do they actually answer the question?

15
00:00:48.866 --> 00:00:51.739
If not, it rewrites the query and retrieves again.

16
00:00:52.539 --> 00:00:56.649
This grade, rewrite, retry loop is the single most valuable addition.

17
00:00:56.979 --> 00:01:01.046
Cap it at two or three rounds, then answer with what you have, or say you can't.

18
00:01:01.846 --> 00:01:06.873
Now Maya asks which suppliers of parts in the recalled batch also ship to EU plants.

19
00:01:07.203 --> 00:01:11.121
No single chunk holds that answer: it's a join across documents.

20
00:01:11.921 --> 00:01:15.326
One fix is iterative decomposition: find the recalled parts,

21
00:01:15.376 --> 00:01:17.802
then their suppliers, then where they ship.

22
00:01:18.132 --> 00:01:20.109
Each hop's result shapes the next search.

23
00:01:20.909 --> 00:01:25.381
The other is GraphRAG: extract entities and relations into a graph up front.

24
00:01:25.711 --> 00:01:29.736
Then the join becomes a graph traversal instead of a similarity search.

25
00:01:30.536 --> 00:01:34.219
But a graph needs a big extraction pipeline and ongoing upkeep.

26
00:01:34.549 --> 00:01:39.129
It pays off only when multi-hop, relational questions are the core workload.

27
00:01:39.929 --> 00:01:43.420
Finally, Maya asks how many frames were returned last quarter.

28
00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:45.225
That lives in a database,

29
00:01:45.275 --> 00:01:48.884
so the agent writes SQL rather than searching embedded rows.

30
00:01:49.684 --> 00:01:52.436
Guard text-to-SQL with a restricted schema,

31
00:01:52.486 --> 00:01:56.802
read-only credentials, validated queries, row limits, and timeouts.

32
00:01:57.132 --> 00:01:59.962
And return the SQL with the answer so it can be checked.

33
00:02:00.762 --> 00:02:05.827
Exam tip: say knowledge graph only when explicit relationships must be traversed,

34
00:02:05.877 --> 00:02:07.027
like supply chains.

35
00:02:07.357 --> 00:02:12.000
For find-the-passage questions it's over-engineering, so read on for the details.
